'''Ōhāriu''' (previously spelled '''Ohariu''' and '''Ōhariu''') is a New Zealand parliamentary [[New Zealand electorates|electorate]] returning one Member of Parliament to the [[New Zealand House of Representatives|House of Representatives]]. It first existed from 1978 to 1993, and again from 2008 until 2026.

The electorate will be abolished ahead of the [[2026 New Zealand general election|2026 general election]].

The current MP for Ōhāriu is [[Greg O'Connor (politician)|Greg O'Connor]] of the [[New Zealand Labour Party|Labour Party]]. He has held this position since 2017.

==Population centres==
Ōhāriu is located in the north of Wellington City, providing representation for the suburbs between [[Crofton Downs]] and [[Tawa, New Zealand|Tawa]], including [[Ngaio, New Zealand|Ngaio]], [[Khandallah]], [[Johnsonville, New Zealand|Johnsonville]] and [[Newlands, New Zealand|Newlands]]. The Lower Hutt suburbs of [[Korokoro, New Zealand|Korokoro]], [[Maungaraki]] and [[Normandale, New Zealand|Normandale]] were in the electorate from 2008 until 2014.

== History ==
The 1977 electoral redistribution was the most overtly political since the Representation Commission had been established through an amendment to the ''Representation Act'' in 1886, initiated by [[Robert Muldoon|Muldoon]]'s [[Third National Government of New Zealand|National Government]].{{sfn|McRobie|1989|pp=8–9, 51, 119}} As part of the [[1976 New Zealand census|1976 census]], a large number of people failed to fill in an electoral re-registration card, and census staff had not been given the authority to insist on the card being completed. This had little practical effect for people on the general roll, but it transferred [[Māori people|Māori]] to the general roll if the card was not handed in. Together with a northward shift of New Zealand's population, this resulted in five new electorates having to be created in the upper part of the [[North Island]].{{sfn|McRobie|1989|p=119}} The electoral redistribution was very disruptive, and 22 electorates were abolished, while 27 electorates were newly created (including Ohariu) or re-established. These changes came into effect for the {{NZ election link|1978}}.{{sfn|McRobie|1989|pp=115–120}}

Ohariu replaced the {{NZ electorate link|Karori}} electorate, but did not include any of [[Khandallah]] or [[Ngaio, New Zealand|Ngaio]].{{sfn|McRobie|1989|pp=114–119}} It ceased to exist in 1990, when it was replaced by the recreated [[Onslow (electorate)|Onslow electorate]].

Ōhariu (now spelled with a leading [[Macron (diacritic)|macron]]) was reinstated for the [[2008 New Zealand general election|2008 general election]]. Its boundaries were very similar to the former [[Ohariu-Belmont (electorate)|Ohariu-Belmont electorate]], except the [[Belmont, Wellington|Belmont]] area (part of [[Lower Hutt]]) was lost to [[Remutaka (electorate)|Rimutaka]] in exchange for Ōhariu picking up [[Crofton Downs]] from {{NZ electorate link|Wellington Central}}. At its re-creation the electorate stretched from Crofton Downs to southern [[Tawa, New Zealand|Tawa]], including [[Ngaio, New Zealand|Ngaio]], [[Khandallah]], [[Johnsonville, New Zealand|Johnsonville]] and [[Newlands, New Zealand|Newlands]]. [[Korokoro, New Zealand|Korokoro]], [[Maungaraki]] and [[Normandale, New Zealand|Normandale]] were included in the electorate until 2014.

[[Peter Dunne]] was the longserving representative of Ōhāriu (and the electorates that came between its abolition and reinstatement) from 1984 until his retirement in 2017.

The 2025 boundary review required a reduction in North Island electorates by one to reflect nationwide population shifts. The three Wellington City electorates ([[Rongotai (electorate)|Rongotai]], [[Wellington Central (electorate)|Wellington Central]] and [[Ōhāriu (electorate)|Ōhāriu]]) were all underpopulated. All were abolished. The population of Ōhāriu was redistributed to {{NZ electorate link|Wellington North}}, [[Kenepuru (electorate)|Kenepuru]] and [[Hutt South]].<ref>{{cite book |url=https://elections.nz/assets/pagecomponent-file-files/REPORT-OF-THE-REPRESENTATION-COMMISSION-2025-WEB.pdf |title=Report of the Representation Commission 2025 |date=8 August 2025 |publisher=Representation Commission |isbn=978-0-473-75233-0 |page=18 |access-date=21 September 2025}}</ref>

